Z||000024||California Kid||10-03-2001||11:35 PM||||I think the smell all of you are talking about is coming from the "Cast Foam" under the Carpet.  Most automakers use a "Cotton Shoddy Absorber Pad" under the carpet, as the cast foam is more expensive. The cast foam slowly "out gases" (not harmful) for many years (don't think it ever really stops).<P>I believe all model years of the Fiero had the cast foam under the carpet. On occasion it's used in other cars/trucks where more dimensional integrity is needed due to numerous formation changes in the floorpan sheetmetal. If anyone has currently got their carpet out of the car, give the cast foam a whif. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||MikeSchaefer||10-04-2001||03:56 AM||||Hmm, here are the components of my 84's smell:<BR><B>Leather Conditioner</B> (on mr.mikes seats)<BR><B>Son of a Gun</B>, from vinyl<BR><B>Hint of that sweet coolant smell</B><BR> (from when the heater core was leaking a long long time ago).<BR>And <B>A touch of stale air</B> right now (hasn't moved in a few months).<BR>You mix all of that together and WOW, it's adds another level to driving the car.  I've sometimes found myself going out to it and just sitting in there when I feel down, it helps me feel better, probably because I associate being in the Fiero with fun, and that smell is part of the sensation.<BR>Later,<BR>Michael Schaefer<P><BR>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cheer_1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by MikeSchaefer (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
